</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As our model predicts camera poses of input 3 frames and future 3 frames, we evaluate the trajectory prediction on KITTI-360 and Waymo open dataset (200 sequences of 6 frames each), and compare it to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> with all 6 frames as input. We report Absolute Trajectory Error (ATE), rotation error (Rot), and translation error (Trans). ATE measures the discrepancy between predicted and ground-truth trajectories after alignment. Rot and Trans denote the mean angular rotation error (deg) and mean translation error (m), respectively. In Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2.3 Trajectory prediction ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Learning to Drive is a Free Gift: Large-Scale Label-Free Autonomy Pretraining from Unposed In-The-Wild Videos" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we can observe that while the metrics are slightly worse that the teacher model, the result is still competitive, considering that our model does not have access to the last 3 frames.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
